				"For the target video, at 0.00 seconds into the target video, <Picture 1> (from [Shot 1]) is fully referenced.\n\nintegrated_multimodal_description:\n\n[Shot 1] Photorealistic live-action cinematic video beginning exactly from <Picture 1>. Fully preserve the 25-year-old East Asian woman, her natural facial identity, chin-length blonde bob with soft bangs, dangling silver earrings, dark navy-blue sleeveless qipao-style dress with embroidered peach and gold floral patterns, body proportions, skin tone, expression, the wooden turntable, transparent dust cover, single black vinyl record, tonearm, wooden console, beige curtains, indoor plant, bright window, city buildings, and sunlight direction. The scene remains elegant, realistic, bright, and peaceful. Preserve the exact initial composition: the woman stands on the left, the turntable is on the right, her left hand is near the power knob, and her right hand is holding the tonearm above the record.\n\nThe video lasts exactly five seconds and uses one continuous stabilized shot.\n\n[0s-0.8s] Begin with the exact still composition of <Picture 1>. The woman remains calm and focused on the turntable. The black vinyl record is perfectly centered and completely stationary. The tonearm is lifted slightly above the outer edge of the record. Her left hand rests beside the power knob and her right hand gently supports the tonearm. Do not change her face, hairstyle, earrings, dress, hands, or the room layout.\n\n[0.8s-1.8s] She slowly rotates the same small power knob once with her left fingers. The movement is precise and restrained. The transparent dust cover, wooden turntable body, record, tonearm, and all surrounding objects remain unchanged.\n\n[1.8s-2.8s] The turntable motor starts with a gradual, realistic acceleration. The single black vinyl record begins rotating smoothly around its exact center without wobbling, duplication, deformation, or sudden speed changes. The woman keeps her right hand steady on the tonearm.\n\n[2.8s-3.8s] She lowers the same tonearm in one slow controlled motion. The stylus approaches the outer groove and makes one precise contact with the record surface. Her hand remains anatomically correct and connected to the tonearm. The tonearm does not float, detach, bend, or pass through the record.\n\n[3.8s-4.5s] The record continues rotating at a stable speed. A subtle warm instrumental melody begins naturally from the turntable, accompanied by delicate vinyl texture. The woman releases the tonearm and withdraws her right hand only a short distance, keeping her arm within the original composition.\n\n[4.5s-5s] She looks down toward the rotating record with a calm, gentle expression. The camera performs only a very subtle forward push of approximately 10 centimeters at a slow constant speed, keeping her face, embroidered dress, tonearm, rotating record, transparent dust cover, and bright window visible together. Hold the stable composition during the final half-second.\n\nCamera and transitions: one continuous stabilized shot beginning from the exact composition of <Picture 1>. Keep the camera nearly static, with only one subtle low-amplitude 10-centimeter forward push during the final part of the shot. No cuts, dissolves, zoom jumps, whip pans, handheld shake, focus jumps, changes of lens perspective, or sudden changes in room geometry.\n\nConstraints: exactly one 25-year-old East Asian woman, one wooden turntable, one transparent dust cover, one black vinyl record, one tonearm, one stylus, and one power knob. No duplicate woman, duplicate record, duplicate tonearm, extra hands, extra fingers, malformed hands, record wobble, record duplication, record deformation, tonearm detachment, floating stylus, clothing changes, hairstyle changes, disappearing earrings, readable labels, visible brand names, subtitles, logos, or watermarks.\n\noverall_soundscape:\n\nQuiet bright-room ambience continues throughout. The power knob produces one restrained mechanical click, followed by a soft turntable motor hum that gradually increases as the platter begins rotating. When the stylus touches the record, a delicate vinyl contact sound and subtle record crackle begin naturally. All sounds remain synchronized with their visible causes. No dialogue, speech, footsteps, or unrelated sound effects.\n\nnon_diegetic_music:\n\nN/A. The warm instrumental melody is diegetic music produced by the vinyl record.",
